\section{Arguments and the sandbox boundary}{

As in \code{system2()}, \code{args} contains already-quoted command-line fragments,
not an argv vector. On Unix, the command, \code{env}, and \code{args} are evaluated by
\verb{/bin/sh -c} \strong{inside} the sandbox. Pipes, substitutions, and redirections
in those fragments therefore do not execute in the launcher's host shell.
On Windows, the target is launched directly; \code{env} is passed after the
command, as in Windows \code{system2()}. It only works with targets such as R
and make that understand those command-line assignments. No \code{cmd.exe}
shell is added. Nonstandard Windows command-line parsers may differ after
Console's argv round trip.

